            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Augustus's Early Life</strong><br>\nGaius Octavius Thurinus, known as Augustus, was born in Rome on September 23, 63 BC. Raised in a wealthy and established family, Augustus was Julius Caesar's great-nephew. After Caesar's assassination, Augustus discovered that Caesar had adopted him as his son in his will. This event transformed young Octavius's life, placing him on a path to political power.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nAugustus's rise to power began with his inheritance of Julius Caesar's wealth and soldiers. He formed a political alliance known as the Second Triumvirate with Mark Antony and Marcus Lepidus in 43 BC. They pursued Caesar's assassins in a series of battles, leading to their defeat. However, the Triumvirate's union was fraught with internal conflict and did not last long.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conflict with Mark Antony</strong><br>\nThe rivalry with Mark Antony was fueled by political and personal conflicts, including Antony's affair with the Egyptian queen, Cleopatra. Augustus cleverly used Antony's relationship with Cleopatra to his advantage, arguing that Antony was more loyal to Egypt than to Rome. This led to the decisive Battle of Actium in 31 BC, where Augustus defeated Antony and Cleopatra's combined forces.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
