            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Becoming Augustus</strong><br>\nAfter his victory, Octavius returned to Rome, where he was hailed as a hero. In 27 BC, he was granted the title \"Augustus\" by the Roman Senate, which marked the beginning of his reign as the first emperor of Rome. With this new title, he claimed he restored the Republic, but he actually held supreme power, effectively ending the era of the Roman Republic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Reforms and Policies</strong><br>\nAugustus implemented a range of reforms that stabilized and enriched Rome. He reorganized the Roman military and created a standing army with soldiers loyal to him. He also established a police force and a firefighting service to improve public safety. Moreover, he introduced legal reforms and renewed the focus on family values.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Building Projects</strong><br>\nKnown for his building projects, Augustus famously said he found Rome a city of bricks and left it a city of marble. He commissioned the construction of roads, temples, and public spaces. The most notable of his projects was the Forum of Augustus, which included the Temple of Mars Ultor, celebrating Rome's avenging spirit.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
