          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Patron of the Arts</strong><br>\nAugustus understood the power of culture and the arts in shaping public opinion and Roman identity. He patronized poets like Virgil and Horace, who created works that linked his rule with Rome's golden age. The era under Augustus's rule is often referred to as the \"Augustan Age,\" considered a high point in Roman literary history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Death</strong><br>\nAugustus's personal life was marked by family tragedies, including the exile of his daughter Julia and the early deaths of his heirs. He was married to Livia Drusilla for over fifty years, who played a significant role in his rule. Augustus died on August 19, 14 AD, after a long illness, leaving behind a legacy that would influence the Roman Empire for centuries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Impact</strong><br>\nAugustus's reign laid the foundation for over two centuries of relative peace in Rome, known as the Pax Romana. His leadership style established a model for future emperors, balancing military power with political acumen. The empire flourished under his rule, expanding its boundaries and establishing efficient governance that endured long after his death.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
